<sect>chains (Revision 1.2 -- 29/05/98)
<p>
<sect1> Type Definition of chain
<p>
<sect2>Struct Node
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node
{
  struct Node *prev;  /* pointer to previous node */
  struct Node *next;  /* pointer to next node */ 
  void *data;         /* void pointer to data */
}
</verb></tscreen>

The structure of a chain can be resumed by the following diagram:

<tscreen><verb>
   nil <- head <--> node1 <--> node2 ....  <--> last node  -> nil
           |          |          |                  |  
          nil       data1      data2              dataN
</verb></tscreen>

In the case of a cyclic chain node1 and last node are connected 
<tt/node1->prev = node last/ and <tt/node last->next = node1/).
<tt/head->next/ still points to <tt/node1/.

<tscreen><verb>
   nil <- head ->    node1 <--> node2 ....  <--> last node  <--> node1
           |           |          |                  |   
          nil        data1      data2              dataN
</verb></tscreen>


<sect1> Memory Allocation of chains 
<p>
All of the functions in this section do memory allocation or
free memory.

<sect2>chain_init
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_init(int *count)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>initialization of  a new chain. <tt/count/ is then number
of elements in the chain (put to <tt/0/).

<sect2>chain_newelement
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_newelement(void * data)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Memory allocation for a new <tt/Node/. 


<sect2>chain_freeelement
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void chain_freeelement(struct Node * p, int removedata)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Frees memory of a <tt/Node/. If <tt/removedata == TRUE, data/ is
also freed, otherwise not. 
It does <bf/not/ check if it was part of a chain and does <bf/not/
remove it from a chain. See also <tt/chain_remove/.

<sect2>chain_free
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void chain_free(struct Node *chain, int removedata, int count)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Frees the memory of the entire chain. If the chain
is cylcic the <tt/head/ of the chain must be passed to
the function otherwise any node will do it.
If <tt/removedata = TRUE/ the pointer <tt/data/
of all nodes are also freed otherwise not. This allows
to free the chain, as a skeleton structure, and to keep
the data. The number of elements in
the chain <tt/count/ is passed to the routine but not changed.


<sect1>Inserting and Deleting nodes
<p>
No function in this section does anything like memory allocation
or freeing, they only deal with the bookkeeping tasks of
a chain.

<sect2>chain_insert
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_insert (struct Node *chain, struct Node *element, int *count)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Inserts the node <tt/elememt/ in the chain, after the node <tt/chain/. 
<tt/count/ represents the number of element in the chain and is incremented
by <tt/1/.


<sect2>chain_append
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_append (struct Node *chain, struct Node *element, int *count)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Inserts the node <tt/elememt/ in the chain as last member of the chain
<tt/count/ represents the number of elements in the chain and is incremented
by <tt/1/.

<sect2>chain_insertsorted
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_insertsorted (struct Node *chain, struct Node *element, 
                                  int (*compare)(), int *count)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Inserts the node <tt/elememt/ in the chain. The chain is supposed to
be sorted and the new node is inserted in the correct place. The order
is defined by the function <tt/compare/, wich takes the data pointer
of 2 nodes as arguments. You must provide this function with the 
following return values: ( returnvalue&gt;0  if node1&gt;node2 --
returnvalue=0 if node1=node2 -- returnvalue&lt;0 if node1&lt;node2).
<tt/count/ represents the number of elements in the chain and is incremented
by <tt/1/.

<sect2>chain_remove
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_remove (struct Node * chain, int *count)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Removes the Node <tt/chain/ from the chain.
<tt/count/ represents the number of elements in 
the chain and is decremented by <tt/1/.

<sect1>Positioning in Chain
<p>

<sect2>chain_head
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_head (struct Node *chain)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Returns the head of the chain. Never use with cyclic chains.

<sect2>chain_last
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_last (struct Node *chain)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Returns the last node of the chain. Never use with cyclic chains.

<sect2>chain_next
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_next (struct Node *chain)  
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Returns the next node of the chain or <tt/NULL/ at the end of the chain.

<sect2>chain_prev
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_prev (struct Node *chain)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Returns the previous node of the chain or <tt/NULL/ at the 
beginning of the chain.

<sect1>Chain to Array Conversion 
<p>
<sect2>chain_to_array
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void * chain_to_array (struct Node * chain, int count)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Converts a chain to an array (with allocation of memory for array 
of pointers)
Returns the array of pointers. 

<sect2>array_to_chain
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * array_to_chain (void **array, int count)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Converts an array to chain (with allocation of memory for the chain)
Returns head of chain.

<sect2>array_free
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void array_free (void **array, int removedata, int count)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>Frees the memory of the array. 
If <tt/removedata = TRUE/ the pointer <tt/data/
of all nodes are also freeed otherwise not. This allows
to free the array of void, as a skeleton structure, and to keep
the data. The number of elements in
the chain <tt/count/ is passed to the routine but not changed.


<sect1>Miscellaneous
<p>
<sect2>chain_cyclic
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_cyclic (struct Node *chain)
</verb></tscreen>
<p>
 Makes the chain cyclic by closing last with first node.
<tt/head/ is returned. It must be saved for freeing the chain.

<sect2>chain_acyclic
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_acyclic(struct Node *head)
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Makes a cyclic chain acylcic. Breaks where header points at.

<sect2>chain_quicksort1
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
void chain_quicksort1(struct Node *node1, struct Node *node2, int (*compare)())
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Sorts the elements from node1 to node2 using quicksort algorithm.
The order is defined by the function <tt/compare/, wich takes the data pointer
of node1 and node2  as arguments. You must provide this function with the 
following return values: ( returnvalue&gt;0  if node1&gt;node2 -- 
returnvalue=0 if node1=node2 -- returnvalue&lt;0 if node1&lt;node2).

<sect2>chain_quicksort2
<p>
<tscreen><verb>
struct Node * chain_quicksort2(struct Node *chain, int (*compare)())
</verb></tscreen>
<p> Sorts the entire chain and returns head of the sorted chain. It uses
<tt/chain_quicksort1/.



